 \begin{enabstract}
     Artificial intelligence is increasingly applied to complex reasoning and content generation. In this dissertation, reasoning encompasses computational processes such as association analysis, compositional logic computation, and visual content generation. These tasks face challenges from growing data volumes, complex computational logic, and limited computing resources. Structural information in data and models can help determine which computations need to be performed, which results can be reused, and how many resources should be allocated to different computations. Analyzing structural regularities within tasks and using them to optimize execution strategies is therefore an important approach to improving reasoning efficiency.
 
-    From the perspective of structure-aware optimization, this dissertation studies three levels: computation range, reasoning orchestration, and resource allocation. Dynamic shortest-path index maintenance, training for complex logical queries over knowledge graphs, and sparse attention inference for video Diffusion Transformers (DiTs) are selected as representative tasks at these levels. The three studies respectively examine how to reduce computation range, organize computation execution, and allocate computing resources according to demand.
+    From the perspective of structure-aware optimization, this dissertation studies three levels: computation range, reasoning orchestration, and resource allocation. Dynamic shortest-path index maintenance, training for complex logical queries over knowledge graphs, and sparse attention inference for video Diffusion Transformers (DiTs) are selected as representative tasks at these levels. The first three studies respectively examine how to reduce computation range, organize computation execution, and allocate computing resources according to demand. A fourth study develops a unified formal framework and validates it through an engineering prototype.
 
     (1) At the computation-range level, this dissertation studies dynamic shortest-path index maintenance. Existing methods precompute information such as distances between vertices to build indexes that accelerate shortest-path queries. In dynamic graphs, edge insertions and deletions may change shortest-path relationships between vertices, requiring indexes to be maintained so that they remain consistent with the updated graph. To address this problem, this dissertation proposes Fully Batch Maintenance (FulBM) for a landmark-based three-hop cover labeling index. The topological structure considered by FulBM is the subgraph formed by vertices whose shortest paths or labels change after edge updates and the associated edges. The extent of this subgraph depends on the effects of edge updates: insertions may shorten distances, whereas deletions may invalidate existing shortest paths, requiring different propagation and repair procedures. Accordingly, FulBM uses separate batch insertion and deletion maintenance algorithms to reduce unnecessary vertex accesses and repeated propagation while keeping the maintained index identical to the index rebuilt on the updated graph.
 
