     (3) At the resource-allocation level, this dissertation studies sparse attention inference for video DiTs and proposes Precision-Allocated Sparse Attention (PASA), a training-free mechanism. The temporal structure of this task is reflected in changes between velocity-field predictions at adjacent denoising steps. The magnitude of these changes varies across denoising stages, as does the demand for exact attention computation. Fixed-sparsity methods such as PISA assign the same sparsity ratio to each denoising step within the sparse interval, making it difficult to accommodate these stage-dependent differences. PASA uses the $L_1$ distance between adjacent velocity-field predictions to set per-step sparsity ratios, allocating more exact attention blocks to steps with larger differences. It combines block selection with approximation of unselected blocks to maintain output fidelity. Experiments show that PASA improves output fidelity under the same exact-computation budget and enables higher average sparsity and lower generation latency at output fidelity comparable to PISA.
 
-    In summary, the three studies exploit topological, logical, and temporal structures to optimize computation range, reasoning orchestration, and resource allocation, respectively. Experimental results show that FulBM achieves speedups of approximately 5.6--228.6$\times$ over BatchHL for mixed batch updates on large-scale graphs, FlashEKGR achieves a speedup of up to 5.67$\times$ over SMORE for multi-hop complex logical-query training, and PASA achieves an average end-to-end inference speedup of 2.02$\times$ over full attention on three video generation models.
+    (4) This dissertation unifies the three mechanisms through a formal framework based on computation dependency graphs, execution scope, node grouping, and computation budgets. An engineering prototype realizes these abstractions as a shared task representation, a common planner, and domain adapters. Simulations on three constructed tasks verify that this execution mechanism preserves the corresponding result constraints. Incremental-query experiments further demonstrate complementary gains from scope reduction and node merging: their composition reduces weighted computation cost by 89.4\% in a representative setting while preserving query outputs.
+
+    In summary, the first three studies exploit topological, logical, and temporal structures to optimize computation range, reasoning orchestration, and resource allocation, respectively. Experimental results show that FulBM achieves speedups of approximately 5.6--228.6$\times$ over BatchHL for mixed batch updates on large-scale graphs, FlashEKGR achieves a speedup of up to 5.67$\times$ over SMORE for multi-hop complex logical-query training, and PASA achieves an average end-to-end inference speedup of 2.02$\times$ over full attention on three video generation models. The fourth study connects these mechanisms through a common cost formulation, shared execution interfaces, and compositional validation.
 
